
Experimental P96 driver for the Minimig FPGA core.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
By Alastair M. Robinson
Huge thanks to the Replay team for releasing their own
P96 driver as open-source - this driver is based heavily
upon their work, and is thus under the same license - LGPL2.1.

Building on Linux
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The assembler must already be installed; it defaults to
~/vbcc/bin/vasmm68k_mot. No assembler or compiler is downloaded.

Run from the repository root:

    make -C amiga_sw/rtg

The default raster is 1024x768 at 66 MHz. To build the alternate driver:

    make -C amiga_sw/rtg RTG_720P=1

This defines RTG_720P for the assembler and selects 1280x720 at 74.25 MHz,
with totals 1650x750, horizontal front porch/sync 110/40 and vertical front
porch/sync 5/5. HDMI, RTG scanout, the board PLL and clock constraints must
be configured for the same raster. The driver does not change FPGA timing.

The alternate exact source widths are 1280 (1x), 640 (2x), and 416 (3x);
heights are 720 (1x), 360 (2x), and 240 (3x). All nine combinations support
CLUT, RGB565 and RGB555. The 416-wide modes occupy 1248 output pixels,
leaving a 16-pixel black border on each side. Noncanonical fallback modes
use the selected profile's canvas thresholds and retain the same limitations.
The .card file still does not install a Picasso96Mode mode list.

Use RTG_720P=0 (or omit it) to restore 1024x768. Changing the option rebuilds
the driver and floppy image without make clean; output filenames are unchanged.
For direct assembly, define RTG_720P only for the 720p build. The raster
constants and RepeatClocks in minimig.card.asm are the place to add profiles.

Serial debug logging is disabled by default. To include it in the driver:

    make -C amiga_sw/rtg DEBUG=1

Use DEBUG=0 (or omit DEBUG) to return to the normal build. Changing this
option rebuilds the driver and floppy image without requiring make clean.
The standalone rtgcheck diagnostic is available in both builds.

Missing Amiga NDK and public/private P96 headers are downloaded into the
ignored .deps directory by download_deps.sh. Subsequent builds reuse them.
To download just the dependencies:

    make -C amiga_sw/rtg deps

This requires GNU make 4.3 or newer, bash, curl, 7z, Python 3 and xdftool
(from amitools). The private
headers supply boardinfo.i and settings.i, while libraries/Picasso96.i
supplies the RGB format constants. The old P96BoardInfo.i/P96CardStruct.i/
P96ModeInfo.i dependencies are unused.

This creates minimig.card, a minimig.card.lst assembler listing, and
minimig-rtg.adf, an 880 KiB OFS floppy image named MinimigRTG. The disk
contains Libs/Picasso96/minimig.card and this README. Insert it in a running
Amiga and copy the driver with:

    Copy MinimigRTG:Libs/Picasso96/minimig.card LIBS:Picasso96/minimig.card

P96 must already be installed; the floppy is a transfer disk, not a boot disk.
The disk also includes the read-only rtgcheck diagnostic. Run
MinimigRTG:rtgcheck from an Amiga Shell to print the RTG ID/status registers,
available 24-bit Fast RAM, installed .card version and its FindCard result.
It does not switch video modes or install interrupts.
Run make adf to generate just the image (building the driver if needed), or
make minimig.card to build only the driver. Override XDFTOOL to select a
different xdftool executable.

Driver tests on the simulated core
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make -C amiga_sw/rtg test

This downloads missing headers using the normal dependency rules, then runs
MMIO tests and both driver profiles (1024x768 and 1280x720). The test target
does not rebuild or replace minimig.card or the ADF. Select one profile with:

    make -C amiga_sw/rtg test TEST_PROFILE=1280x720

Requirements: installed vasm, Python 3, GHDL with Verilog synthesis output,
and Icarus Verilog (iverilog/vvp). Driver build include-path/VBCC overrides
also apply to tests. No FPGA build, Amiga ROM, AmigaOS or P96 runtime is needed.

The test source is in tests/. It translates the current TG68K VHDL wrapper
and kernel to Verilog once per suite, then runs actual driver callback code
as a 68020 test ROM against the current RTG and Akiko Verilog modules. ROM
and RAM responders are behavioral. The shared hardware/MMIO bench and its
assembly test ROM are in ../../bench/rtg_hdmi/.

Each driver profile checks 26 exact, fallback, zero and oversized mode cases
in CLUT, RGB565 and RGB555, plus split limits for all vertical repeat factors.
Checks include repeat selection, pixel clocks, blanking/sync values, display
switching, aligned stride, native fallback and split-register writes. MMIO
checks include ID reads, address decode, longword base/palette writes and
register readback through the actual CPU bus handshake.

This tests callback routines extracted from minimig.card.asm, not the complete
.card library lifecycle or Picasso96Mode. Scanout stays inactive; these tests
do not boot Workbench, render HDMI, exercise SDRAM arbitration or test CDC.
The corresponding pixel, SDRAM, IRQ and serializer benches remain in bench/.

To reuse a CPU netlist you generated yourself, pass CPU_NETLIST=/path/cpu.v.
Otherwise the suite always translates the current sources. Run directly with:

    python3 amiga_sw/rtg/tests/run.py
    python3 amiga_sw/rtg/tests/run.py --mmio-only

Generated ROMs, CPU netlists and executables go into automatically removed
temporary directories. tests/archive/ preserves earlier driver reproductions;
these target older interfaces and are not part of the current passing suite.

Library vector offsets are generated from the NDK FD files with Python 3. Override
VBCC, NDK_DIR, P96_PRIVATE or P96_PUBLIC on the make command line to use
other installations. The output requires a 68020 or newer CPU.

Dependency downloads:
https://os.amigaworld.de/index.php?lang=en&page=12
https://wiki.icomp.de/wiki/P96#Software_Developer_archive

V1.1 adds support for screen dragging, removing a couple of ugly hacks
and solving the issue of resyncing when flipping screens.

V1.2 adds support for a VIDEOMEMORY tooltype which can be set to an
integer between 1 and 7 to select how many megabytes the driver reserves
for RTG.  (Please note, if the driver can't allocate a large enough chunk
it will halve the requested size and try again until it succeeds.

V1.3 has a few bug fixes, and now supports vertical autoscrolling.
(Horizontal autoscrolling remains unsupported.)

V1.4 adds a few more pixel clocks (slower ones) to allow for the creation
of lower-res screenmodes suitable for games. (The scandoubler currently
remains unavailable in RTG mode, however.)


Installation
~~~~~~~~~~~~

V1.13 adds the optional RTG_720P build profile. Default builds retain the
1024x768 mode set and register layout. Use the matching FPGA raster and
create the selected profile's exact source modes in Picasso96Mode.

V1.12 keeps nonzero clock/timing values for unsupported resolutions, avoiding
the previous zero-MHz rejection during mode editing. For nonzero dimensions
within 1024x768, it selects repetition independently: widths up to 320 use
3x, up to 512 use 2x, otherwise 1x; heights up to 256 use 3x, up to 384 use
2x, otherwise 1x. Zero-sized or oversized modes cannot enable RTG scanout.
No HDL or register-layout change is required.

This is a best-effort display fallback, not additional exact hardware modes.
Stride follows the actual bitmap pitch, but hardware still reads a complete
1024/512/320 by 768/384/256 canvas. Outside a smaller image it may display
data from later rows or beyond the bitmap allocation; those areas are not
guaranteed black. For example, 320x240 uses 3x3 repetition and reads 16 extra
source rows. Prefer the nine exact modes below, especially for Workbench.

V1.11 removes bitmap canvas-width expansion, supports only the advertised
CLUT, RGB565 and RGB555 formats, and disables serial debug logging by default.
It uses the same register layout as V1.10 and requires no further core update.

V1.10 removes the unused frame-sequence diagnostic and compacts the RTG
control registers. Install the matching core and driver together; older
versions use different control-register addresses. The legacy ID and status
registers retain their addresses.

V1.9 adds independent 1x, 2x and 3x pixel repetition with the matching core.
The HDMI output remains 1024x768 at 66 MHz. Supported source widths are
1024 (1x), 512 (2x), and 320 (3x); supported heights are 768 (1x), 384 (2x),
and 256 (3x). Any width/height combination from those lists is supported.
For example, 1024x384 uses 1x horizontally and 2x vertically. The 320-wide
modes produce 960 output pixels with a 32-pixel black border on each side.

P96 2.0 allocation callbacks do not provide the mode or bitmap height.
The exact supported modes therefore use complete source canvases. V1.12
also allows the imperfect fallback described above, without replacing the
P96 memory allocator. For example, 960x384 uses 1x2 repetition, but hardware
still reads 1024 source pixels per output row.
CalculateBytesPerRow aligns the actual bitmap row size to 16 bytes, including
small offscreen bitmaps. SetPanning programs the actual stride. A displayed
screen bitmap must cover the full hardware canvas width and height for a
correct image. Horizontal panning is not supported. Dragged screens must share
format and repetition settings.

Picasso96Mode settings supply the mode list; the .card driver does not
install one. Create the supported combinations there. The logical clock
and timing callbacks describe scaled modes; they do not reprogram HDMI.
Vertical repetition rereads each output row from SDRAM, so it requires no
additional row RAM and does not reduce SDRAM traffic by itself.

New registers relative to $B80100:
  $14: bits 1:0 horizontal, bits 3:2 vertical; 0=1x, 1=2x, 2=3x.
  $16: source stride in 16-byte bursts; zero selects the visible row size.
Changes take effect when the next frame is prepared.

V1.8 restores BIF_VBLANKINTERRUPT and requires the matching FPGA update.
The core now supplies frame interrupts from the selected HDMI pixel clock
in both native and RTG modes, so P96 can receive its first interrupt before
switching the display. Framebuffer DMA remains inactive in native mode.

V1.7 is a diagnostic build that does not advertise BIF_VBLANKINTERRUPT to
P96. It tests whether waiting for an interrupt before RTG scanout starts
causes the mode-test hang. HDMI vblank polling remains available. No FPGA
change is required when updating from V1.6.

V1.6 requires the matching FPGA core. RTG uses fixed 1024x768 HDMI timing
at 66 MHz. It does not program Agnus timing or BEAMCON0. Native video is
selected at reset and until P96 explicitly switches to RTG.

The RTG-owned word registers are:
  $B80110: display switch, bit 0 (0 native / 1 RTG), read/write.
  $B80112: secondary framebuffer's first display row, read/write;
           $7ff disables the screen split. Rows are HDMI active-area rows.
Framebuffer, pixel-format, palette and HDMI vblank registers retain their
existing addresses. The clock controller resets HDMI during mode switching;
that reset does not clear the requested display or framebuffer registers.

* Install P96 using its installer.  You'll be prompted to pick a card
  (any card!) - it shouldn't matter which you choose, but I'll
  assume for the rest of these instructions that you chose PicassoIV.

  The P96 installer will create a new monitor file in Devs:Monitors

* Rename the newly-created "PicassoIV" monitor file to "Minimig"

* Edit the monitor file's tooltypes, changing the BOARDTYPE tooltype
  to minimig then save the changes.

* Optionally add a tooltype, VIDEOMEMORY=<n> to set the amount of used
  video memory to <n> megabytes.

* Copy the minimig.card file to libs:picasso96/

* Optionally, copy the Picasso96Settings file to Devs:
  (The Picasso96Settings file contains some pre-defined screenmodes
  which all work nicely on my Dell U2311H monitor - but may need
  tweaking, or just outright fail to work on other monitors.)

* Reboot, and you should be able to run Picasso96Mode to try out the
  various screenmodes, or select them from within applications.

